# Director of Public Safety — Independent Brief

**Run:** `quiet-by-design-standards-for-mwaa-terminals-2`
**Chair:** Director of Public Safety, large-hub US airport authority (Police, ARFF/Fire, 911/Dispatch under one chain of command)
**Written for:** Council synthesis into the MWAA quiet-by-design thesis for IAD and DCA
**Date:** 2026-08-07

---

## How I am reading this thesis

The thesis argues MWAA should adopt a common quiet-by-design standard across IAD and DCA — and that "quiet" cannot collapse into "fewer announcements." Read from the public-safety chair, that framing is either the best thing that will happen to emergency communications at these two airports in a decade, or the fastest way to walk into a stampede on a Saturday afternoon. Which one it becomes depends on whether the standard treats life-safety broadcast as a mandatory floor with force-of-law engineering behind it, or as one of several requirements to be traded off in value engineering after the design is 60% complete.

I run a police department, a fire and ARFF department, and a dispatch center. In an incident, none of us can outrun the speed of information to 40,000 people on the floor. The public address system is the fastest tool we have to move a crowd. Take it seriously in the standard, and quiet-by-design will make my incident commanders more effective, not less. Treat it as an architectural preference and it will bury a life-safety asset inside a design manual chapter about acoustics.

## Key findings

- **Speech intelligibility is not a design preference — it is a national fire code requirement.** NFPA 72 (2022) requires that emergency communication in each acoustically distinguishable space (ADS) meet an average Speech Transmission Index (STI) of at least 0.50, with no measurement location below 0.45 at 90% of points [Source: https://legalclarity.org/nfpa-72-the-national-fire-alarm-and-signaling-code-explained/]. Any MWAA quiet-by-design standard that does not name STI 0.50/0.45 and the ADS commissioning discipline behind it is not enforceable; it is aspirational.
- **The applicable design bible for airport PA already exists and is publicly domestic.** ACRP Research Report 175 gives measurable performance thresholds — STI targets, signal-to-noise ratio of 10 to 15 dB, reverberation and finish guidance — and identifies high reverberation as the top intelligibility complaint from airport operators [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/]. MWAA should not be inventing thresholds. It should be binding ACRP 175 and NFPA 72 into its Design Manual.
- **Reducing baseline announcement frequency and preserving mass-notification capability are two different engineering problems.** A "silent airport" policy at Schiphol or Copenhagen changes what the operator broadcasts, not what the physical system is capable of broadcasting when the fire panel or dispatch keys the mic. Any MWAA standard must specify both, and specify that public safety override — dispatch, incident commander, senior duty officer — cannot be defeated by announcement suppression logic. This is the failure mode the branding-only version of "quiet" imports by accident.
- **The second-wave stampede is the real risk, and it is a communications risk.** At Fort Lauderdale on 6 January 2017, the initial shooting killed five and wounded six. Roughly 90 minutes later, false reports of additional gunfire produced widespread panic; more than 50 additional people were hospitalized during that stampede [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]. The cascading injuries were caused by an absence of authoritative information reaching a crowd already in motion. A quiet-by-design standard that hardens zoned all-call broadcast, tenant override, and directive messaging is a stampede-prevention standard. One that quietly lowers the baseline SPL without hardening those channels is the opposite.
- **ADA-compliant visual redundancy is the actual floor for "visual-first," not an upgrade.** NFPA 72 and the International Building Code already require visible notification (strobes, textual/video displays) in parallel with audible emergency communication, so that deaf, hard-of-hearing, or noise-masked occupants are notified [Source: https://acl.gov/ada/alarms-people-who-are-deaf-or-hard-hearing]. If MWAA specifies "visual-first" in a policy document while its actual dynamic-signage network is not integrated with the mass-notification panel and does not commission to ADA reach and contrast, we have inverted the compliance model.
- **MWAA already has the sworn depth and command discipline to enforce a serious standard — and the political altitude to require it.** The Airports Authority Police Department has 233 sworn officers with Virginia certification, concurrent jurisdiction with Arlington, Fairfax, and Loudoun, and jurisdiction over the Dulles Toll Road [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority]. MWAA Fire and Rescue Department fields 163 personnel across four stations and provides ARFF, structural, technical rescue, hazmat, EMS, and river rescue [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority_Fire_and_Rescue_Department]. Under a director of public safety model, the standard signs out over one chain of command; that is the vehicle to make life-safety broadcast requirements binding on architects, tenants, and airlines.
- **Post-crash DCA is the wrong moment to lower the messaging baseline.** The 29 January 2025 midair collision, subsequent FAA helicopter route changes near DCA, and the runway 15/33 and 4/22 restrictions have materially raised the irregular-operations messaging load at DCA. A quiet standard that fails to preserve surge capacity — the physical ability to key a directive message to every gate on a diversion night — will be judged against a memory of 67 fatalities, not against a peer-airport branding case.

## Evidence base

### Fire code and intelligibility standards

Two references govern voice-based life safety in a US terminal, and they are already law of the land inside every certificated occupancy MWAA operates:

- NFPA 72 (2022 edition), Chapter 24, requires intelligibility in every acoustically distinguishable space in an emergency communications system. The measurable definition is at least 90% of measurement locations at STI ≥ 0.45 with an average of ≥ 0.50 per ADS [Source: https://legalclarity.org/nfpa-72-the-national-fire-alarm-and-signaling-code-explained/]. Practically, that means the standard's design chapter has to require designers to (a) declare every ADS on drawings, (b) commission STI at TCO with an approved test method, and (c) re-commission after any tenant fit-out that changes finishes, occupancy, or noise floor. If the standard names STI without naming ADS declaration and re-commissioning, tenants will re-carpet a hold room and quietly walk the space out of compliance.
- ACRP Research Report 175, "Improving Intelligibility of Airport Terminal Public Address Systems," is the domestic reference and is FAA-sponsored. It sets a target signal-to-noise ratio of 10 to 15 dB, catalogs the finish and geometry problems (glass, polished stone, high vaulted volumes) that IAD's Main Terminal and any AeroTrain platform will fight against, and identifies reverberation as the top driver of operator-reported complaints [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/].

The relevant international reference underneath both is IEC 60268-16 (STI-PA measurement method) and IEC 60849 / EN 54-16 (voice alarm equipment). MWAA specifications already edit from MASTERSPEC; binding the STI test method and the ADS discipline is a specification amendment, not a new regulatory regime.

### Federal ARFF and airport policing floor

- 14 CFR §139.319 requires ARFF response within 3 minutes from alarm to the midpoint of the farthest runway serving air carriers with a first vehicle beginning agent application; all other required vehicles at 4 minutes [Source: https://www.law.cornell.edu/cfr/text/14/139.319]. That is the airfield floor and it is unaffected by anything in a quiet-by-design standard — but it is the reason terminal PA and dispatch integration matters: an on-airfield incident cascades into terminal communications almost immediately (holds, diversions, evacuation of stands adjacent to gates), and the dispatcher and the incident commander need one intelligible pathway from the ARFF net to the terminal all-call.
- The MWAA police force, at 233 sworn, enforces Airports Authority regulations, county code in Arlington, Fairfax, and Loudoun, and Virginia state law, with concurrent jurisdiction from the surrounding counties [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority]. That jurisdictional structure means MWAA is not renting a police response from a host municipality; it is running one. A standard imposed by MWAA public safety on its own facility has the enforcement backbone behind it.
- MWAA Fire and Rescue Department, 163 personnel and four stations spanning both airports, is the ARFF and structural fire authority [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority_Fire_and_Rescue_Department]. Any MWAA standard that touches life-safety broadcast has to be co-signed by the fire code official.

### Incident evidence

- Fort Lauderdale, 6 January 2017. Five killed, six wounded at initial contact. Roughly 90 minutes later, false reports of additional gunfire triggered a stampede across all four terminals; more than 50 additional people were treated at hospitals for chest pain, fractures, and injuries sustained while fleeing [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf]. The after-action report from the Police Executive Research Forum / National Policing Institute documents communication and command-post lockdown challenges during the response window [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/uncategorized/fort-lauderdale-hollywood-international-airport-active-shooter-incident-and-post-event-response/].
- LAX, 1 November 2013. TSO Gerardo Hernandez was killed at a checkpoint; two other officers and a passenger wounded. The federal record is now the TSA Administrator's House Homeland Security testimony, which established annual active-shooter training as an agency mandate and confirmed that a 350-officer joint exercise had been held three weeks before the incident [Source: https://www.tsa.gov/news/press/testimony/2014/03/28/lessons-lax-shooting-preparing-and-responding-emergencies-airports]. LAX Airport Police Chief Gannon's testimony to lawmakers made the operational thesis explicit — response speed is the operative variable, and the tools that scale with speed are radio, dispatch, and PA [Source: https://www.kpcc.org/news/2014/02/28/42532/lax-shooting-lessons-airport-police-chief-tells-la/].
- The 2025-2026 sequence of unattended-bag and bomb-threat closures across US airports (Miami, JFK, LAX, RDU, KCI, Houston, Nashville) is the routine background rate against which any MWAA standard will be tested [Source: https://www.cbsnews.com/miami/news/miami-international-airport-evacuations-suspicious-item-luggage/]. These are the events where dispatch, police, fire, and terminal ops key the PA in rapid succession, sometimes concurrently.

### Accessibility and visual redundancy

- ADA and NFPA 72 together already require visible notification in parallel with audible in publicly occupied buildings, and the FAA has directed airports to ensure facilities are accessible to deaf, deafblind, and hard-of-hearing travelers, including through visual paging and hearing loops [Source: https://acl.gov/ada/alarms-people-who-are-deaf-or-hard-hearing]. Any "visual-first" language in an MWAA standard must integrate the dynamic-signage network with the fire alarm and mass-notification panel and commission to reach, contrast, and dwell-time targets, not simply promise that "screens will display" during an emergency.

### MWAA-specific governance and current context

- MWAA maintains a seven-volume 2020 Design Manual described as a mandatory guide with the force of law on airport property, updated annually, edited from MASTERSPEC (from run context packet §7). This is the vehicle. A quiet-by-design standard adopted as a new or amended Design Manual volume with an accompanying specification section and commissioning appendix is enforceable against consultants and contractors on every subsequent MWAA capital project; a policy paper without that binding is not.
- The 29 January 2025 midair collision and subsequent FAA action (helicopter route removals near DCA, restrictions on runways 15/33 and 4/22) have raised DCA's ambient IROPS messaging load (from run context packet §8). The post-collision NTSB final report identified multiple systemic failures across FAA, ATC, and military operations. In that environment, "we broadcast less" is a difficult sentence for MWAA leadership to be quoted saying, absent a defensible engineering standard behind it.

## The public safety case for the thesis

The case for is stronger than most public safety executives will initially concede. Announcement clutter is itself a life-safety problem. Passengers habituate to constant PA activity, tune it out, and cannot distinguish an important message from noise when the moment comes. Reverberant terminal spaces — the Saarinen Main Terminal at IAD, glass-and-stone concourses in general — degrade intelligibility precisely because they are over-broadcasted, layered with adjacent-zone spillover, and never commissioned against measurable STI. ACRP 175's own data shows this: operators identify high reverberation and layered messaging as their leading intelligibility complaints [Source: https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/24839/].

A quiet-by-design standard, properly written, forces the discipline my incident commanders need. Declaring ADS on drawings, requiring STI commissioning at TCO, zoning the PA so a gate-hold announcement in Concourse C does not spill into a fire evacuation in the adjacent bag hall, and hardening a public-safety mic priority path — these are the exact things that make emergency communications work when it matters. Done at design phase they cost pennies against the program. Argued as change orders after commissioning they cost millions and often lose. The window MWAA is standing in — Concourse E opening, C/D replacement scoped, DCA T1 replacement inside the use-and-lease capital plan — is the once-in-a-generation window to bind this discipline before the drawings are 60%. If the Council does its job, "quiet-by-design" is the operational-safety upgrade that finally gets approved because it is dressed as passenger experience.

## The public safety case against the thesis

The case against is not a case against a standard — it is a case against a bad one. A standard written in the language of passenger experience without STI, ADS, mass-notification integration, and tenant override provisions will be treated by the AE community as an architectural preference, sacrificed in value engineering, and interpreted by tenants as license to install decorative acoustic treatments and quieter PA architecture that cannot deliver an intelligible directive broadcast to a moving crowd. That is the Fort Lauderdale failure mode — not too much PA, but a PA architecture that could not project authority to a panicked terminal at the moment it needed to.

The transfer risk from international peers is real and public safety executives should say it plainly. Schiphol's, Helsinki's, and Copenhagen's silent-airport programs sit inside different life-safety regulatory regimes, different labor and dispatch models, and terminal geometries that are meaningfully different from IAD's Main Terminal or a DCA T1 hold room. The reported "90 minutes of noise removed" and "40% reduction" at SFO have not been verified in primary sources in the run's context packet, and I would not commit MWAA capital or standards language to unverified peer metrics. If the Council wants to invoke peer practice, it should say what those airports actually engineered — not what they announced.

The other honest caveat is cost. High-STI zoned MNS with integrated visual notification, tenant override, and public-safety mic priority at IAD and DCA scale is not free. The right number is an all-in premium of low single-digit percent on affected capital, not a rounding error, and it will show up as commissioning, systems integration, tenant coordination, and post-occupancy verification. It is defensible. It is smaller than one wrongful-death settlement. But the Council should not pretend the premium does not exist, and MWAA is entering the tightest coverage window of its capital cycle (context packet §3) — the standard has to survive that finance conversation on its merits.

## Operational realities a strategist can use

1. **NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 / ≥ 0.45 / 90% is the floor. ADS discipline is the enforcement mechanism.** Any MWAA standard that names STI without requiring per-ADS declaration on drawings, TCO commissioning, and re-commissioning after tenant fit-out will not survive its first Concourse C tenant build-out. This is the sentence I would put in the Design Manual first.
2. **Public-safety mic priority must be undefeatable.** Dispatch, incident commander, and senior duty officer paths must ride on top of any automated announcement suppression, tenant PA, or gate-podium override. This is trivial to specify at design phase and unforgiving to retrofit.
3. **Tenant systems are the failure mode.** Airline clubs, concessions, gate podiums, and airline agent PA are where zoning and override break in practice. The standard has to bind tenant lease language, not just Design Manual language — that is why the airline use-and-lease integration matters, and why counsel and MWAA Real Estate belong on the working group.
4. **Fort Lauderdale, LAX, and the ongoing bag/threat closures are the case reference set.** FLL is the stampede lesson. LAX is the mandated-annual-training and speed-of-response lesson. The 2025–2026 unattended-bag sequence is the routine load the system must carry without operator fatigue [Source: https://www.policinginstitute.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/Ft.-Lauderdale-Hollywood-International-Airport-AAR.pdf][Source: https://www.tsa.gov/news/press/testimony/2014/03/28/lessons-lax-shooting-preparing-and-responding-emergencies-airports].
5. **MWAA carries the enforcement backbone in-house.** 233 sworn police with concurrent jurisdiction and 163 fire/EMS across four stations serving both airports gives the director of public safety a real chain of command to hold consultants, contractors, and tenants to a serious standard [Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority][Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Washington_Airports_Authority_Fire_and_Rescue_Department]. This is not true at every peer authority. Use it.

## Bottom line

Quiet-by-design is the right conversation. It is also the conversation where compliance and competence get confused, and where a well-intentioned architectural preference can quietly disable the fastest information channel my incident commanders own. Bind NFPA 72 STI ≥ 0.50 and ADS discipline. Bind ACRP 175 as the design reference. Bind public-safety mic priority. Bind tenant override. Bind visual redundancy to ADA and NFPA 72 by name. Do all of that inside the existing MWAA Design Manual and Specifications architecture. Commission at TCO and re-commission at fit-out. Then, and only then, reduce baseline announcement frequency where the operations case supports it. That is a standard I can defend to a grand jury after a bad night. The version that leads with "fewer announcements" and hopes the engineering follows is a standard I cannot.
